    <h1>Asynchronous Programming in Java</h1>

    <p>
        Asynchronous programming ka purpose ye hai ki application ek task ke
        complete hone ka unnecessarily wait na kare. Isse independent tasks ko
        efficiently execute karna possible hota hai.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>1. Synchronous vs Asynchronous Programming</h2>

    <h3>Synchronous</h3>

    <p>
        Synchronous execution me tasks one-by-one execute hote hain.
        Pehla task complete hone ke baad next task start hota hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>task1();
task2();
task3();</code></pre>

    <h3>Asynchronous</h3>

    <p>
        Asynchronous execution me ek task background me execute ho sakta hai
        aur main program doosra work continue kar sakta hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>startTask1();

doOtherWork();

waitForTask1();</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>2. Why Asynchronous Programming?</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Application responsiveness improve hoti hai.</li>
        <li>Independent tasks efficiently execute ho sakte hain.</li>
        <li>I/O operations ke dauran unnecessary waiting reduce hoti hai.</li>
        <li>Server applications me scalability improve ho sakti hai.</li>
        <li>Background processing ke liye useful hai.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>3. Thread and Asynchronous Execution</h2>

    <p>
        Java me asynchronous execution ko achieve karne ke liye threads,
        Executor Framework aur CompletableFuture jaise mechanisms available hain.
    </p>

    <pre><code>Thread task = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Task is running");
});

task.start();</code></pre>

    <p>
        <code>start()</code> call karne par task separate thread par execute hota hai.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>4. Creating a Thread</h2>

    <p>
        Java me thread create karne ka simple way <code>Thread</code> class
        use karna hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread thread = new Thread(() -&gt; {

            System.out.println(
                "Running in: " +
                Thread.currentThread().getName()
            );

        });

        thread.start();
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Lambda expression ki wajah se separate <code>Runnable</code> class
        likhne ki zarurat nahi hai.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>5. Runnable Interface</h2>

    <p>
        <code>Runnable</code> ek functional interface hai jisme
        <code>run()</code> method hota hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>Runnable task = () -&gt; {

    System.out.println(
        "Task executed"
    );

};

Thread thread =
    new Thread(task);

thread.start();</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>6. Thread.sleep()</h2>

    <p>
        <code>Thread.sleep()</code> current thread ko specified duration ke
        liye pause karta hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>try {

    Thread.sleep(2000);

} catch (InterruptedException e) {

    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();

}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Yaha <code>2000</code> milliseconds = <strong>2 seconds</strong>.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>7. join()</h2>

    <p>
        <code>join()</code> ka use current thread ko kisi doosre thread ke
        complete hone tak wait karane ke liye hota hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>Thread worker = new Thread(() -&gt; {

    System.out.println("Worker started");

});

worker.start();

try {

    worker.join();

} catch (InterruptedException e) {

    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();

}

System.out.println("Worker completed");</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>8. Executor Framework</h2>

    <p>
        Directly bahut saare threads create karne ke bajay Java ka
        <strong>Executor Framework</strong> thread management ko simplify karta hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ExecutorService executor =
            Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        executor.submit(() -&gt;
            System.out.println("Task 1")
        );

        executor.submit(() -&gt;
            System.out.println("Task 2")
        );

        executor.submit(() -&gt;
            System.out.println("Task 3")
        );

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>9. ExecutorService</h2>

    <p>
        <code>ExecutorService</code> tasks ko execute aur manage karne ke
        liye use hota hai.
    </p>

    <p>Common methods:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><code>submit()</code> - task submit karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>execute()</code> - Runnable execute karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>shutdown()</code> - new tasks accept karna stop karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>shutdownNow()</code> - currently running tasks ko interrupt karne ki request karta hai.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>10. Callable</h2>

    <p>
        <code>Runnable</code> result return nahi karta. Agar asynchronous task
        se result chahiye, to <code>Callable</code> use kar sakte hain.
    </p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.Callable;

Callable&lt;Integer&gt; task = () -&gt; {

    return 10 + 20;

};</code></pre>

    <p>
        Callable checked exception bhi throw kar sakta hai.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>11. Future</h2>

    <p>
        <code>Future</code> asynchronous task ke future result ko represent karta hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args)
            throws Exception {

        ExecutorService executor =
            Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

        Future&lt;Integer&gt; future =
            executor.submit(() -&gt; {

                return 100;

            });

        System.out.println(
            future.get()
        );

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        <code>get()</code> result available hone tak wait kar sakta hai.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>12. CompletableFuture</h2>

    <p>
        <code>CompletableFuture</code> Java me asynchronous programming ke
        liye powerful API hai. Isse asynchronous tasks ko chain aur combine
        karna easy hota hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        CompletableFuture&lt;String&gt; future =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -&gt; "Hello Java"
            );

        future.thenAccept(
            result -&gt;
                System.out.println(result)
        );
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Output:</strong></p>

    <pre><code>Hello Java</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>13. supplyAsync()</h2>

    <p>
        <code>supplyAsync()</code> asynchronous task execute karta hai aur
        result return karta hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; future =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
        () -&gt; 10 + 20
    );</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>14. runAsync()</h2>

    <p>
        Agar asynchronous task ko result return nahi karna hai to
        <code>runAsync()</code> use kar sakte hain.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt; future =
    CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -&gt; {

        System.out.println(
            "Background task"
        );

    });</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>15. thenApply()</h2>

    <p>
        <code>thenApply()</code> previous asynchronous result ko transform
        karne ke liye use hota hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; future =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
        () -&gt; 10
    );

CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; result =
    future.thenApply(
        value -&gt; value * 2
    );

result.thenAccept(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Output:</strong></p>

    <pre><code>20</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>16. thenAccept()</h2>

    <p>
        <code>thenAccept()</code> result ko consume karta hai but new result
        return nahi karta.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -&gt; "Java")
    .thenAccept(
        value -&gt;
            System.out.println(value)
    );</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>17. thenCompose()</h2>

    <p>
        Jab ek asynchronous operation ke result ke basis par another
        asynchronous operation start karna ho, <code>thenCompose()</code>
        useful hota hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;String&gt; result =
    CompletableFuture
        .supplyAsync(() -&gt; "User")
        .thenCompose(
            value -&gt;
                CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                    () -&gt; value + " Details"
                )
        );

result.thenAccept(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>18. Combining Async Tasks</h2>

    <p>
        Multiple independent asynchronous tasks ko combine bhi kar sakte hain.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; first =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
        () -&gt; 10
    );

CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; second =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
        () -&gt; 20
    );

CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; result =
    first.thenCombine(
        second,
        (a, b) -&gt; a + b
    );

result.thenAccept(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Output:</strong></p>

    <pre><code>30</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>19. Exception Handling with CompletableFuture</h2>

    <p>
        Async pipeline me exceptions handle karne ke liye
        <code>exceptionally()</code> use kar sakte hain.
    </p>

    <pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; future =
    CompletableFuture
        .supplyAsync(() -&gt; {

            throw new RuntimeException(
                "Something went wrong"
            );

        })
        .exceptionally(
            error -&gt; {
                System.out.println(
                    error.getMessage()
                );
                return 0;
            }
        );

future.thenAccept(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>20. Practical Example: Async Data Processing</h2>

    <pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        CompletableFuture&lt;String&gt; user =
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -&gt; "Rahul"
            );

        CompletableFuture&lt;String&gt; result =
            user.thenApply(
                name -&gt;
                    "Welcome " + name
            );

        result.thenAccept(
            System.out::println
        );
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Output:</strong></p>

    <pre><code>Welcome Rahul</code></pre>

    <hr />

    <h2>21. Thread Pool</h2>

    <p>
        Thread pool pre-created threads ka group hota hai jo multiple tasks
        execute kar sakta hai.
    </p>

    <pre><code>ExecutorService executor =
    Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);</code></pre>

    <p>
        Common thread pool types:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><code>newFixedThreadPool()</code></li>
        <li><code>newCachedThreadPool()</code></li>
        <li><code>newSingleThreadExecutor()</code></li>
        <li><code>newScheduledThreadPool()</code></li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>22. Asynchronous Programming Use Cases</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Database operations</li>
        <li>REST API calls</li>
        <li>File processing</li>
        <li>Background jobs</li>
        <li>Email processing</li>
        <li>Message processing</li>
        <li>Multiple independent service calls</li>
        <li>Large data processing</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>23. Important Best Practices</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Unnecessarily new Thread create na karein.</li>
        <li>Thread pools ke liye Executor Framework prefer karein.</li>
        <li>ExecutorService use karne ke baad shutdown karein.</li>
        <li>Shared mutable data ko carefully handle karein.</li>
        <li>Blocking <code>get()</code> ka unnecessary use avoid karein.</li>
        <li>CompletableFuture chains me exceptions handle karein.</li>
        <li>CPU-intensive aur I/O-intensive tasks ko appropriately manage karein.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>24. Important Interview Points</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Runnable result return nahi karta.</li>
        <li>Callable result return kar sakta hai aur checked exception throw kar sakta hai.</li>
        <li>Future asynchronous task ka result represent karta hai.</li>
        <li>CompletableFuture asynchronous task chaining support karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>supplyAsync()</code> result return karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>runAsync()</code> result return nahi karta.</li>
        <li><code>thenApply()</code> result transform karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>thenAccept()</code> result consume karta hai.</li>
        <li><code>thenCompose()</code> dependent asynchronous operations ke liye useful hai.</li>
        <li><code>thenCombine()</code> independent asynchronous results combine karta hai.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h2>25. Summary</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Asynchronous programming waiting time ko efficiently manage karta hai.</li>
        <li>Java me Thread, ExecutorService, Future aur CompletableFuture important tools hain.</li>
        <li>Executor Framework thread management simplify karta hai.</li>
        <li>CompletableFuture modern asynchronous programming ke liye powerful API hai.</li>
        <li>Async tasks ko chain, combine aur error-handle kiya ja sakta hai.</li>
    </ul>

</div>